{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Behold, while I am still alive with you.</b> This may be explained according to the saying of the Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 18a) that the righteous are called alive even in their death. Thus his saying ״while I am still alive with you״ means: while my life is still with you — from which you learn that afterwards too he is alive, only he is not with them." "And his saying <b>today</b> alludes that he has with them only this day left. And the plain sense of the verse is ״until today.״" "And his saying <b>you have been</b> — in the past tense, for it is possible that they entertained thoughts of repentance at that very moment. And then, regarding one who betroths a woman on condition that he is a complete tzaddik — even if it turns out that he was a complete wicked man, we say that she is betrothed, for perhaps he entertained thoughts of repentance at that very moment (Kiddushin 49b)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:57.865993599Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}